<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tegernsee Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century–<a href="/years/1803">1803</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tegernsee Abbey was a Benedictine monastery on the Tegernsee lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-bavaria">Upper Bavaria</a>, founded in the mid-eighth century by members of a leading Bavarian noble family. It became one of the richest and most learned abbeys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, with a celebrated scriptorium, and the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> romance Ruodlieb was probably written there in the eleventh century. It was secularised in 1803.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tegernsee-abbey.jpg" alt="Tegernsee Abbey" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schloss_Tegernsee.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The early history of the abbey, officially St. Quirinus Abbey, is obscure, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> incursions of the early 10th century and a fire in about 970 destroyed earlier evidence. The community was founded in 746 beside an existing Church of Our Saviour by the brothers Otkar and Adalbert of the Huosi, one of the five old ruling clans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. It was consecrated in 754 and settled by monks from St. Gall. The founders obtained relics of the Roman martyr Quirinus from Pope Paul I. A later tradition, which cannot be verified, held that the brothers withdrew from the world after a son of Pippin the Younger killed Otkar's son with a chessboard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the fall of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tassilo-iii-of-bavaria">Tassilo III of Bavaria</a> the abbey became a Carolingian royal monastery, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-invasions-of-europe">Hungarian raids</a> and the confiscations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnulf-duke-of-bavaria">Arnulf, Duke of Bavaria</a>, weakened it. Emperor Otto II refounded it as an Imperial Abbey in 978 with monks from St. Maximin's Abbey, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>. Under the monk Froumund and Abbot Ellinger it became a centre of literature, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> production and learning; its workshops made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stained-glass">stained glass</a> for Bishop Gottschalk of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a> and cast the doors of Mainz Cathedral in 1014. This golden age lasted almost to the end of the 12th century and produced, besides the Ruodlieb, the Play about the Antichrist and the Tegernsee love letters.</p><p>From 1189, when Manegold of Berg was made abbot through the intrigues of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-andechs">Counts of Andechs</a>, noble families came to dominate the house, and it declined in the 13th and 14th centuries; it burned down in 1410. A visitation in 1426 began a revival, and with the support of Cardinal Nikolaus von Kues the abbey became a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melk">Melk</a> reform, opening the house to a wider range of social classes. Its scribes compiled the Liber illuministarum around 1500, it had its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press from 1573, and it survived a raid by Swedish soldiers in the Thirty Years' War.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The abbey was dissolved on 17 March 1803. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-joseph-of-bavaria">Maximilian I Joseph of Bavaria</a> acquired the buildings in 1817, and as Schloss Tegernsee they passed to the Dukes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, a branch of the Wittelsbach family, who still own them. The former abbey church is the parish church of St. Quirinus, and the site also houses a grammar school and the Ducal Bavarian Brewery of Tegernsee.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tegernsee Abbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ducal monasteries such as <b>Tegernsee</b> and Kremsmünster became among the largest landowners in the duchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
